  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nA blocky upper level pattern continues across the country next\nfew days with strong cutoff low/trofs over the Mid Atlantic \ncoast and West coast early this week and upper level ridge in \nbetween over the nations heartland. Quiet/nice early fall \nweather will likely continue across central/se IL through at \nleast Tuesday night and may even extend into Wednesday as the \nwestern upper level trof is slower to shift eastward into the \ncentral Rockies/central high plains on Wed. More active pattern\nevolves over the region Wed night into Friday as system in\nwestern states moves into the Midwest with a frontal boundary\nalong with remnants of Polo affecting the area late this work \nweek.\n\nMid level convergence driven band of isolated to scattered\nshowers over eastern Iowa central/eastern MO, AR and west\ncentral and far sw IL near MS river valley from near\nJacksonville and south and sw. Jacksonville airport picked up\n0.13 inch rain this morning. Latest CAMs continue to diminish \nthese light showers next few hours over areas from Jacksonville \nsw with dry conditions expected over area by sunset. Some of the\nCAM members like HRRR, RAP and NAM3km show patchy fog (possibly\ndense) developing from I-74 northeast and near the IN border \nduring overnight into early Monday morning. Lows overnight in\nthe upper 40s/lower 50s, coolest east of I-55 where less cloud\ncover. \n\nUpper level ridge builds into IL through Tue with temperatures\ngradually warming up into mid to upper 70s Monday and mostly\nlower 80s Tue (upper 70s over Knox and Stark counties). Lows in\nthe low to mid 50s Monday night, coolest in east central IL. Even\nwarmer on Wed with southerly flow with highs ranging from upper\n70s by Galesburg to mid to upper 80s in southeast IL. We have \n20-40% chance of showers from Quincy to Bloomington north/nw on \nWed with isolated thunderstorms from Galesburg nw. Highs Thu\nstill in mid 80s in southeast IL while low to mid 70s from IL\nriver nw. \n\nThe strong upper level trof near the West Coast to shift\neastward into the central Rockies and central High Plains by Wed\nwhile picking up remnants of Hurricane Polo with showers and a\nfew thunderstorms becoming more likely overnight Wed night over\nthe IL river valley and over rest of CWA on Thu, though\nsoutheast IL may not see likely pops until Thu night/Friday.\nHurricane Polo with 125 mph winds forecast by NHC to track NE\ninto southern Baja of Mexico late Monday afternoon and weaken as\nit moves across the Baja peninsula and reach the nw Mexican \ncoast late Mon night. Its remnants reach near El Paso Texas by \nsunset Tue and its moisture likely to move into IL for the \nThu/Fri time period. NBM pops have lowered from 2-3 inches to \n1.5-2 inches as the system appears slower moving in and and \nfaster moving out Friday afternoon/evening as upper level trof \nopens up. Severe thunderstorm risk still appears low late this \nwork week though moderate to heavy rains still a risk as WPC \nDay5 for Thu/Thu night has slight risk of excessive rainfall \nover central IL and marginal risk in southeast IL. \n\nA 1027-1030 mb high pressure settles into the western Great\nLakes Friday night and eastern Great Lakes by sunset Sat \nbringing drier wx back to the area Fri night and Sat. Models\ndiffer with handling next southern stream system early next\nweek with ECMWF much wetter over CWA on Sunday while GFS and GEM\nmodels keep moisture south of CWA. NBM has 20-30% pops se of the\nIL river on Sunday. Cooler more fall like temps return Friday\nthrough Sunday with highs in the mid 60s to lower 70s and lows\nin the low to mid 50s.",
